          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Is there a case where two conditions documented together must NOT be linked by the coder?",
          "answer": "Yes. The retrieved coding guidance describes several cases where two documented conditions must not both be coded, or must be coded in a specific way.\n\n- **Excludes1 notes:** The classification uses these when two conditions cannot both be present. The codes are never reported together. (Source: ICD-10-CM code structure and conventions.)\n- **Excludes2 notes:** These are weaker. The excluded condition is not part of the code above it, so a person can have both, and both may be reported. (Source: ICD-10-CM code structure and conventions.)\n- **Asthma exacerbation and status asthmaticus:** These are alternatives, not a scale. If status asthmaticus is documented, the code says status asthmaticus and does not also say acute exacerbation. (Source: Coding asthma: J45 and its severity and exacerbation axes.)\n- **Reflux and hiatal hernia:** These are different conditions, so a record with both carries two codes from two blocks. The guidance does not treat one as part of the other. (Source: Coding gastroesophageal reflux disease: K21.)\n- **Hypertension with organ involvement:** When heart or kidney involvement is documented, the classification prefers a combination code instead of a plain hypertension code, so the separate codes are not used in the same way. (Source: Coding hypertension: I10.)\n\nThe passages do not show the full text of the Excludes1 definition or the rule about the word \"with,\" so I cannot give you the complete rule set from them. Coding decisions should be checked against the current official ICD-10-CM tables and guidelines, or with a certified coder.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
